@node Lexical Analysis
@section Lexical Analysis

The Pascal lexer (@code{pascal-lex-source}) converts the input character
stream into a sequence of tokens. It recognizes keywords, operators,
literals, identifiers, and punctuation.

@subheading Keywords

Keywords are case-insensitive and conventionally written in lowercase.
The following keywords are recognized by the lexer:

@table @asis
@item @code{and} - Logical AND operator
@item @code{array} - Array type declaration (syntax only)
@item @code{begin} - Block start
@item @code{case} - Case statement start
@item @code{copy} - Copybook inclusion
@item @code{div} - Integer division
@item @code{do} - Loop body marker
@item @code{end} - Block end
@item @code{endcase} - Case statement end
@item @code{exit} - Exit from loop or procedure
@item @code{fault} - Fault logging
@item @code{for} - For loop
@item @code{goback} - Return from procedure
@item @code{goto} - Unconditional jump
@item @code{if} - Conditional statement
@item @code{in} - Library or bank reference
@item @code{input} - Input statement
@item @code{library} - Library routine modifier
@item @code{log} - Logging statement
@item @code{method} - Method declaration
@item @code{move} - Assignment (alternate syntax)
@item @code{next} - Loop body marker
@item @code{not} - Logical NOT operator
@item @code{of} - Case clause marker
@item @code{on} - Object reference
@item @code{or} - Logical OR operator
@item @code{procedure} - Procedure declaration
@item @code{print} - Print statement
@item @code{protocol} - Protocol declaration
@item @code{read} - Read statement
@item @code{return} - Return statement (alternate)
@item @code{step} - For loop step
@item @code{stop} - Stop program
@item @code{then} - Conditional marker
@item @code{to} - For loop target
@item @code{until} - Post-test loop condition
@item @code{var} - Variable declaration
@item @code{while} - Pre-test loop
@item @code{write} - Write statement
@item @code{dialogue} - Dialogue statement
@end table

@subheading Operators

The following operators are recognized:

@table @asis
@item @code{+} - Addition
@item @code{-} - Subtraction
@item @code{*} - Multiplication
@item @code{/} - Division
@item @code{=} - Equality or assignment
@item @code{<>} - Not equal
@item @code{<} - Less than
@item @code{>} - Greater than
@item @code{<=} - Less than or equal
@item @code{>=} - Greater than or equal
@item @code{:} - Label separator or type qualifier
@item @code{.} - Record field access
@end table

@subheading Comments

Pascal supports three comment styles:

@example
@{ This is a brace comment @}
(* This is a parenthesized-star comment *)
// This is a line comment
@end example

Line comments extend to the end of the line. Lines beginning with @code{;}
are treated as comment lines and ignored.

@subheading String Delimiters

Strings may be delimited by single quotes or double quotes:

@example
'Hello, World!'
"Also valid string syntax"
@end example

@node Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A Pascal program or procedure consists of a procedure declaration with a
body delimited by @code{BEGIN} and @code{END}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
PROCEDURE MyProcedure
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
PROCEDURE InitializeGame
BEGIN
  Score := 0
  LivesLeft := 3
END
@end example

@example
PROCEDURE MainLoop
BEGIN
  ProcessInput
  UpdateGame
  RenderDisplay
END
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Procedure names must be valid identifiers (alphanumeric and underscore)
@item At least one statement is required between BEGIN and END
@item Empty procedures are allowed (no statements)
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item The parser produces a program node containing a method node
@item The method node contains the list of statements
@end itemize

@node Procedures and Methods
@section Procedures and Methods

Procedures define named blocks of code that can be called from other parts
of the program. Methods are procedures associated with a class or object.

@subheading Standalone Procedure

@example
PROCEDURE DoSomething
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@subheading Method on Object

@example
PROCEDURE Move ON Player
BEGIN
  X := X + DeltaX
  Y := Y + DeltaY
END
@end example

The @code{ON} keyword associates the procedure with a specific object or
class. The generated method name becomes @code{ClassName#MethodName}.

@subheading Library Procedure

@example
PROCEDURE DrawSprite IN LIBRARY
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

Library procedures are called with the @code{:library} flag set.

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Procedure identifier: valid identifier name
@item Object identifier (after ON): valid identifier name
@item Body: zero or more statements
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Method node with name: @code{ProcedureName} for standalone
@item Method node with name: @code{ClassName#MethodName} for methods
@end itemize

@node Variables and Types
@section Variables and Types

Variables are declared using the @code{VAR} keyword. Type declarations are
simplified in this dialect.

@subheading Syntax

@example
VAR variableName : type
VAR score : INTEGER
VAR name : STRING
@end example

@subheading Basic Types

@table @asis
@item @code{INTEGER} - Signed integer value
@item @code{REAL} - Floating-point value
@item @code{STRING} - Character string
@item @code{BOOLEAN} - True/false value
@item @code{CHAR} - Single character
@end table

@subheading Examples

@example
VAR counter : INTEGER
VAR playerX : INTEGER
VAR message : STRING
VAR isActive : BOOLEAN
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: alphanumeric with underscores, case-insensitive
@item Type names: recognized type keywords
@end itemize

@subheading Note

The current parser treats variable declarations semantically but the
grammar focuses on executable statements. Variable storage allocation is
handled by the code generation backend.

@node Expressions
@section Expressions

Expressions combine literals, variables, and operators to produce values.

@subheading Primary Expressions

@example
42                    @r{Integer literal}
3.14159              @r{Real literal}
"Hello"              @r{String literal}
myVariable           @r{Identifier}
@end example

@subheading Parenthesized Expressions

@example
(x + y)
((a - b) * c)
@end example

@subheading Record Field Access

@example
player.X
sprite.Position.Y
data.Field1.Field2
@end example

@subheading Array Subscript Access

@example
scores[0]
matrix[row][col]
items[index]
@end example

@subheading Arithmetic Expressions

@example
x + y                @r{Addition}
x - y                @r{Subtraction}
x * y                @r{Multiplication}
x / y                @r{Division}
@end example

@subheading Relational Expressions

@example
x = y                @r{Equal}
x <> y               @r{Not equal}
x < y                @r{Less than}
x > y                @r{Greater than}
x <= y               @r{Less or equal}
x >= y               @r{Greater or equal}
@end example

@subheading Logical Expressions

@example
a AND b              @r{Logical AND}
a OR b               @r{Logical OR}
NOT a                @r{Logical NOT}
@end example

@subheading Operator Precedence

@enumerate
@item Parentheses: highest precedence
@item Unary: NOT, unary minus
@item Multiplicative: *, /
@item Additive: +, -
@item Relational: =, <>, <, >, <=, >=
@item Logical AND
@item Logical OR: lowest precedence
@end enumerate

@node Assignment
@section Assignment

Variables are assigned values using the assignment operator (@code{:=})
or the @code{MOVE} keyword.

@subheading Standard Assignment

@example
@var{variable} := @var{expression}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
Score := 0
Counter := Counter + 1
Total := Price + Tax
Message := "Game Over"
@end example

@subheading MOVE Assignment

The @code{MOVE} keyword provides an alternate syntax for assignment:

@example
MOVE @var{expression} TO @var{variable}
@end example

@example
MOVE 10 TO Score
MOVE X + Y TO Total
@end example

@subheading Array Element Assignment

@example
Scores[0] := 100
Matrix[Row][Col] := Value
@end example

@subheading Record Field Assignment

@example
Player.X := 160
Player.Y := 100
Sprite.Frame := 0
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Target: variable name, array element, or record field
@item Expression: any valid expression
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST node: @code{(:move :from @var{expr} :to @var{target})}
@end itemize

@node Control Structures
@section Control Structures

Conditional execution is provided through @code{IF/THEN/ELSE} and
@code{CASE/OF/ENDCASE} statements.

@subheading IF/THEN/ELSE

@example
IF @var{condition} THEN
  @var{then-statements}
ELSE
  @var{else-statements}
END
@end example

@subsubheading Simple IF

@example
IF Score > 100 THEN
  Bonus := Bonus + 10
END
@end example

@subsubheading IF with ELSE

@example
IF Lives > 0 THEN
  ContinueGame
ELSE
  GameOver
END
@end example

@subsubheading Nested IF

@example
IF Temperature > 100 THEN
  Status := "HOT"
ELSE IF Temperature > 50 THEN
  Status := "WARM"
ELSE
  Status := "COLD"
END
@end example

@subheading CASE Statement

@example
CASE @var{expression} OF
  @var{value1} : @var{statements}
  @var{value2} : @var{statements}
  ELSE : @var{default-statements}
ENDCASE
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
CASE Direction OF
  1 : MoveNorth
  2 : MoveSouth
  3 : MoveEast
  4 : MoveWest
  ELSE : Beep
ENDCASE
@end example

@example
CASE KeyPressed OF
  0 : Score := Score + 10
  1 : Score := Score + 20
  2 : Score := Score + 50
ENDCASE
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Condition: any boolean expression
@item Case values: integer literals
@item ELSE clause: optional
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item IF: @code{(:if :condition @var{cond} :then @var{then-stmts} :else @var{else-stmts})}
@item CASE: @code{(:evaluate :subject @var{expr} :when-clauses @var{clauses})}
@end itemize

@node Loops
@section Loops

Five loop constructs are supported: pre-test (@code{WHILE}), post-test
(@code{UNTIL}), post-test with pre-condition (@code{DO/NEXT/WHILE}),
post-test with post-condition (@code{DO/NEXT/UNTIL}), and counting
(@code{FOR}).

@subheading WHILE Loop

Pre-test loop that continues while condition is true:

@example
WHILE @var{condition} NEXT
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@example
WHILE Lives > 0 NEXT
  ProcessGameFrame
END
@end example

@subheading UNTIL Loop

Post-test loop that continues until condition becomes true:

@example
UNTIL @var{condition} NEXT
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@example
UNTIL KeyPressed = ESC NEXT
  UpdateAnimation
END
@end example

@subheading DO/NEXT/WHILE Loop

Post-test loop with pre-condition check:

@example
DO NEXT WHILE @var{condition}
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

The body executes at least once, then repeats while the condition is true.

@subheading DO/NEXT/UNTIL Loop

Post-test loop with post-condition check:

@example
DO NEXT UNTIL @var{condition}
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

The body executes at least once, then repeats until the condition becomes true.

@subheading FOR Loop

Counting loop with optional step:

@example
FOR @var{variable} FROM @var{start} TO @var{end} STEP @var{step} NEXT
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
FOR I FROM 1 TO 10 NEXT
  Score := Score + 10
END
@end example

@example
FOR X FROM 0 TO 79 STEP 2 NEXT
  DrawPixel X, Y
END
@end example

@example
FOR Index FROM Length DOWNTO 0 STEP -1 NEXT
  Items[Index] := 0
END
@end example

@subheading Exit from Loop

Use @code{EXIT} to break out of a loop:

@example
FOR I FROM 1 TO 100 NEXT
  IF Items[I] = Target THEN
    EXIT
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item WHILE/UNTIL: condition expression
@item FOR: variable name, start/end expressions, optional step expression (default: 1)
@item Step can be positive or negative
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item WHILE: @code{(:perform :until @var{cond})}
@item UNTIL: @code{(:perform :until (NOT @var{cond}))}
@item FOR: @code{(:perform :varying @var{var} :from @var{start} :by @var{step} :until @var{cond})}
@end itemize

@node Procedure Calls
@section Procedure Calls

Procedures are called using the @code{CALL} keyword or by using the
procedure name directly as a statement.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL @var{procedure-name}
@var{procedure-name}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
CALL InitializeGame
ClearScreen
LoadLevel CurrentLevel
PlaySound Explosion
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Procedure name: valid identifier
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST node: @code{(:call :target @var{name})}
@end itemize

@node Method Invocation
@section Method Invocation

Methods on objects are invoked using the @code{CALL ... ON} syntax.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL @var{method-name} ON @var{object-name}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
CALL Move ON Player
CALL Draw ON SpriteManager
CALL Update ON GameState
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Method name: valid identifier
@item Object name: valid identifier referring to an object instance
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST node: @code{(:invoke :class @var{object} :method @var{method})}
@end itemize

@node Library Calls
@section Library Calls

Procedures in external libraries or different memory banks are called
using the @code{CALL ... IN} syntax.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL @var{procedure-name} IN @var{bank-number}
CALL @var{procedure-name} IN LIBRARY
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
CALL LoadBank1 IN 1
CALL DrawSprite IN LIBRARY
CALL PlayMusic IN 3
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Procedure name: valid identifier
@item Bank number: integer literal (for bank calls)
@item LIBRARY keyword (for library calls)
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Bank call: @code{(:call :target @var{name} :bank @var{bank})}
@item Library call: @code{(:call :target @var{name} :library T)}
@end itemize

@node Input and Output
@section Input and Output

Input and output statements provide basic I/O capabilities.

@subheading PRINT Statement

Output a value or string:

@example
PRINT @var{expression}
PRINT "@var{string}"
@end example

@example
PRINT Score
PRINT "Level Complete!"
PRINT "Score: " + Score
@end example

@subheading WRITE Statement

Alternate syntax for output:

@example
WRITE @var{expression}
WRITE "@var{string}"
@end example

@subheading INPUT Statement

Read input into a variable:

@example
INPUT @var{variable-name}
@end example

@example
INPUT PlayerName
INPUT Guess
@end example

@subheading READ Statement

Alternate syntax for input:

@example
READ @var{variable-name}
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item PRINT/WRITE: expression or string literal
@item INPUT/READ: variable name
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item PRINT/WRITE: @code{(:print :expressions (@var{expr}))}
@item INPUT/READ: @code{(:input :variables (@var{var}))}
@end itemize

@node Error Handling
@section Error Handling

Error handling is provided through fault logging and program termination.

@subheading LOG FAULT Statement

Log a fault condition:

@example
LOG FAULT @var{error-code}
@end example

@example
LOG FAULT 1
LOG FAULT ErrorCode
@end example

@subheading STOP Statement

Terminate program execution:

@example
STOP @var{exit-code}
@end example

@example
STOP 0
STOP ExitCode
@end example

@subheading EXIT Statement

Exit from current loop or procedure:

@example
EXIT
@end example

@example
FOR I FROM 1 TO 100 NEXT
  IF Found THEN
    EXIT
  END
END
@end example

@subheading GOBACK Statement

Return from procedure:

@example
GOBACK
@end example

@example
PROCEDURE CheckBounds
BEGIN
  IF X < 0 THEN
    GOBACK
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item LOG FAULT: integer expression
@item STOP: integer expression
@item EXIT/GOBACK: none
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item LOG FAULT: @code{(:log-fault :code @var{code})}
@item STOP: @code{(:stop-run :code @var{code})}
@item EXIT: @code{(:exit-method)}
@item GOBACK: @code{(:goback)}
@end itemize

@node Dialogue and Narrative
@section Dialogue and Narrative

The @code{DIALOGUE} statement displays narrative text, typically for game
storytelling.

@subheading Syntax

@example
DIALOGUE "@var{text}"
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
DIALOGUE "Welcome to the game!"
DIALOGUE "You found a treasure chest."
DIALOGUE "The door creaks open slowly."
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Text: string literal
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST node: @code{(:dialogue :speaker "narrator" :text @var{text})}
@end itemize

@node Copybook Inclusion
@section Copybook Inclusion

Copybooks are source files containing shared declarations that can be
included in multiple programs.

@subheading Syntax

@example
COPY "@var{filename}";
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
COPY "common/types.pas";
COPY "game/constants.pas";
@end example

@subheading Note

The semicolon after the filename is optional.

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Filename: string literal with file path
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST node: @code{(:copy :name @var{filename})}
@end itemize

@node Record Types
@section Record Types

Records group related data items of different types. While the current
parser does not have explicit record type declarations, record field
access is supported through the dot operator.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{record-variable}.@var{field}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
Player.X := 100
Player.Y := 50
Item.Data.Count := 0
@end example

@subheading Nested Records

@example
Customer.Address.City := "Springfield"
Customer.Address.Zip := 12345
@end example

@subheading Record Assignment

@example
Player := NewPlayer
Player.X := ScreenWidth / 2
Player.Y := ScreenHeight / 2
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Record variable: variable of record type
@item Field: identifier naming a field within the record
@end itemize

@subheading Note

Record type definitions are not explicitly parsed; the backend handles
record layout and field access. See the COBOL frontend for copybook-based
record definitions.

@node Arrays
@section Arrays

Arrays provide indexed access to collections of elements of the same type.

@subheading Single-Dimensional Arrays

@example
@var{array}[@var{index}]
Scores[0]
Items[Index]
Data[Count]
@end example

@subheading Multi-Dimensional Arrays

@example
@var{array}[@var{dim1}][@var{dim2}]
Matrix[Row][Col]
Table[X][Y][Z]
@end example

@subheading Array Assignment

@example
Scores[0] := 100
Items[I] := Items[I] + 1
Matrix[Row][Col] := Value
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Index: integer expression
@item Multiple indices for multi-dimensional arrays
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Subscript access: @code{(:subscript @var{array} @var{index})}
@end itemize

@subheading Note

Array declarations and bounds checking are handled by the backend.
The frontend supports subscript access syntax only.

@node Operators Reference
@section Operators Reference

@subheading Arithmetic Operators

@table @asis
@item @code{+} - Binary addition; combines two values
@item @code{-} - Binary subtraction; subtracts right from left
@item @code{*} - Multiplication; multiplies two values
@item @code{/} - Division; divides left by right
@end table

@subheading Relational Operators

@table @asis
@item @code{=} - Equality; true if operands are equal
@item @code{<>} - Inequality; true if operands differ
@item @code{<} - Less than
@item @code{>} - Greater than
@item @code{<=} - Less than or equal
@item @code{>=} - Greater than or equal
@end table

@subheading Logical Operators

@table @asis
@item @code{AND} - Logical AND; true if both operands true
@item @code{OR} - Logical OR; true if either operand true
@item @code{NOT} - Logical NOT; inverts boolean value
@end table

@subheading Assignment Operators

@table @asis
@item @code{:=} - Assignment; assigns value to variable
@item @code{MOVE} - Alternate assignment syntax
@end table

@subheading Precedence

Highest to lowest:
@enumerate
@item Parentheses
@item Unary (NOT, -)
@item Multiplicative (*, /)
@item Additive (+, -)
@item Relational (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=)
@item Logical AND
@item Logical OR
@end enumerate

@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The following Pascal features are not supported in this frontend:

@table @asis
@item @strong{File I/O} - @code{assign}, @code{reset}, @code{rewrite}, @code{close}, @code{blockread}, @code{blockwrite}
@item @strong{Graphics} - @code{graph}, @code{initgraph}, @code{setcolor}, @code{circle}, @code{line}, @code{rectangle}
@item @strong{Pointer types} - Pointer declarations and pointer arithmetic
@item @strong{Sets} - Set types and set operations
@item @strong{With statement} - @code{WITH record DO} construct
@item @strong{Labeled GOTO} - @code{GOTO label} (labels without the statement structure)
@item @strong{Procedural parameters} - Procedures as parameters to other procedures
@item @strong{Function results} - Function return value assignment
@item @strong{Type declarations} - Explicit type definitions (@code{TYPE T = ...})
@item @strong{Enumerated types} - @code{(red, green, blue)}
@item @strong{Subrange types} - @code{1..10}
@item @strong{Exception handling} - @code{TRY}, @code{EXCEPT}, @code{FINALLY}, @code{RAISE}
@item @strong{Units and modules} - @code{INTERFACE}, @code{IMPLEMENTATION}, @code{USES}
@item @strong{Inline assembly} - @code{ASM} blocks
@item @strong{Procedural variables} - Variable procedures
@item @strong{Records with methods} - Methods inside records (use classes instead)
@item @strong{Class operators} - Operator overloading
@item @strong{Generics} - Generic types and procedures
@item @strong{Anonymous methods} - Inline procedure literals
@item @strong{Property definitions} - @code{PROPERTY} keyword
@item @strong{Exception handling} - @code{TRY}, @code{EXCEPT}, @code{FINALLY}
@item @strong{Preprocessor} - @code{$DEFINE}, @code{$IFDEF}, @code{$INCLUDE}
@end table

@subheading Alternative Approaches

@itemize @bullet
@item For file I/O: Use CALL to library routines
@item For graphics: Use CALL to graphics library routines
@item For records: Access fields using dot notation
@item For types: Use COBOL copybooks via COPY statement
@end itemize
